// Windows process provider (#3332, epic #3322): process exit, command-line
// arguments and environment variables — the Bit form of
// `../linux/os.bit`/`../darwin/os.bit`'s own `bit_rt_os_*` role, but a
// DIFFERENT SPLIT OF LABOUR from both, because Windows hands the process none
// of what POSIX hands it for free.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY THIS FILE OWNS MORE THAN ITS SIBLINGS DO
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// On Linux and Darwin, `os_argc`/`os_arg_at`/`os_env` are PLATFORM-FREE
// (`../os.bit`) because the kernel hands the process an `argc`/`argv`/`envp`
// triple already shaped exactly like the C convention `../os.bit`'s
// `gArgc`/`gArgvPtr`/`gEnvpPtr` and `osCstrToStr`/`osEnvMatch` assume: a word
// array of pointers to NUL-terminated byte strings, and — since both
// platforms' terminals and shells already deal in UTF-8 — those bytes are
// already the right encoding. `../{linux,darwin}/os.bit` only add
// `os_self_exe`/`os_run`/`os_run_test`, the three calls that need a real
// syscall the shared code cannot make itself.
//
// Windows has NO such triple. `../boot.bit`'s own header: "there is no
// per-process argc/argv/envp AT ALL. A Windows program obtains its command
// line via `GetCommandLineW` and its environment via
// `GetEnvironmentStringsW` — both UTF-16, neither shaped like the C
// `envp: [*:0]?[*:0]u8` array". So THIS file's job is to MANUFACTURE that
// same C-shaped, UTF-8-byte triple from the two UTF-16 Windows APIs, once, at
// boot — and once manufactured, `../os.bit`'s `osSetArgs`/`rtOsArgc`/
// `rtOsArgAt`/`rtOsEnv` need NOT ONE LINE changed: they already only ever
// read raw bytes through a pointer array, never caring how those bytes came
// to exist. `../boot.bit`'s `boot()` (step 3) now calls `winCaptureArgsEnv`
// below instead of the placeholder empty-array `osSetArgs` call its header
// documented as "#3332's job" — see that file's diff in this same commit.
//
// `os_exit` (`bit_rt_os_exit`) lives HERE rather than in `./io.bit` (the
// `../{linux,darwin}/io.bit` location), because `../boot.bit`'s own
// `winSpawnFatal` comment already reserves this file for it: "that symbol
// belongs to `runtime/root/windows/os.bit` (#3332), which owns real
// process-exit semantics on Windows."
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE UTF-16 / UTF-8 BOUNDARY — `winUtf16ToUtf8` AND `winUtf8ToUtf16` BELOW
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Every wide-string Windows API this port will ever call crosses this same
// boundary, so both directions live in exactly one place: `winUtf16ToUtf8`,
// used by `winSplitArgs` (command line) and `winBuildEnvp` (environment)
// below, and `winUtf8ToUtf16` (#3334, `../fs.bit` — same module, so `fs.bit`
// calls it with no import needed), used to encode a Bit `string` path for
// `CreateFileW`/`DeleteFileW`/`CreateDirectoryW`/`RemoveDirectoryW`/
// `MoveFileW`/`GetFileAttributesW`/`SetFileAttributesW`/`FindFirstFileW`.
// `fs.bit` also reuses `winUtf16ToUtf8` a second time, for
// `FindFirstFileW`/`FindNextFileW`'s returned `cFileName` — the decode
// direction this file already established, not a second copy of it.
//
// MALFORMED UTF-16 (an unpaired surrogate) DECODES AS U+FFFD, never rejected
// outright and never passed through as WTF-8: Bit strings are always
// well-formed UTF-8 (SPEC's `string`), so this function's output must be too
// no matter how corrupt the input block is — the standard substitution
// WHATWG's own UTF-16 decoder and every major language runtime make for
// exactly this input. A well-formed astral-plane character (a real surrogate
// PAIR) round-trips exactly, verified below.
//
// VERIFIED IN ISOLATION, NOT ON THIS TICKET'S FAITH ALONE: this port cannot
// build or run for `x86_64-windows` yet (`libbitrt-x86_64-windows.a` does not
// exist, #3341), so `winUtf16ToUtf8`/`winEncodeUtf8Cp` and `winSplitArgs`'s
// quoting state machine were each mirrored line-for-line in a throwaway
// Python script and checked against Python's own UTF-16LE/UTF-8 codecs and
// against Microsoft's four canonical documented command-line examples
// ("Parsing C++ Command-Line Arguments") before being written here — the
// mutation-in-isolation technique for logic that is too expensive (here:
// impossible on this host) to exercise directly. Not committed; this comment
// is the durable record. Runtime behaviour on real Windows is still OWED —
// see the bottom of this file.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// COMMAND-LINE SPLITTING FIDELITY — A DELIBERATE, STATED DEVIATION
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `winSplitArgs` implements the three rules Microsoft documents for
// `CreateProcess`'s `lpCommandLine` (the same rules the C runtime's
// `__getmainargs` and `CommandLineToArgvW` both implement): arguments are
// delimited by space/tab outside a quoted run; a `"` toggles the quoted run
// and is itself never copied; a run of N backslashes immediately before a
// `"` collapses to floor(N/2) literal backslashes, and an ODD N additionally
// turns the `"` into one literal, non-toggling `"` character; backslashes
// NOT immediately before a `"` are always literal, in full.
//
// APPLIED UNIFORMLY TO EVERY ARGUMENT, INCLUDING ARGV[0] — the one place this
// deliberately diverges from `CommandLineToArgvW`, which special-cases the
// executable-path token (delimited by the first unquoted whitespace, or by a
// matching close-quote if it starts with one, with NO backslash escaping
// applied inside it at all). Replicating that second, path-only state
// machine buys fidelity for an input nothing plausible produces — a
// program's own launched path containing a literal `\"` sequence — at the
// cost of a second parser to keep in sync with this one. This ticket's own
// acceptance criteria test arguments AFTER argv[0] (plain, quoted-with-a-
// space, embedded-quote), which get the fully faithful treatment either way.
// A half-faithful splitter that only worked on `prog.exe hello` would be
// worse than this documented gap.

import { osSetArgs } from "../../root"
// `loadByte`/`storeByte`/`storeWord` are NOT imported here: an import in Bit is
// per-MODULE, not per-file, and `./io.bit` (#3331) already imports all three
// from "../../gc" for this module. A second import of the same name is E0042,
// which `git merge` cannot see — #3331 and this ticket were written in parallel
// off the same base and each was green alone.

// kernel32 imports beyond what `../boot.bit` already declares (same module,
// so `ExitProcess` below is `../boot.bit`'s own extern, not redeclared here —
// a second `extern fn ExitProcess` would be E0042). All three of these are
// already admitted in `compiler/pesyms.bit` (#3328) — no addition needed for
// this ticket. `GetCommandLineW`/`GetEnvironmentStringsW` return a raw
// pointer (`*byte`, this project's idiom for "untyped memory", exactly how
// `../boot.bit`'s own `CreateThread` returns its `*byte` HANDLE) to a UTF-16
// buffer; nothing about the calling convention cares what the pointee type
// is, only the callee does.
extern fn GetCommandLineW(): *byte
extern fn GetEnvironmentStringsW(): *byte
extern fn FreeEnvironmentStringsW(strings: *byte): i32

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// process exit
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// `bit_rt_os_exit` (ABI.md §19): every compiled panic and `os.exit()` call
// funnels here, exactly as `../linux/io.bit`'s and `../darwin/io.bit`'s own
// `rootExit`. `../boot.bit`'s `winMain` ALSO calls `ExitProcess` directly for
// its own top-level return — that is a SEPARATE call site (boot's own
// post-`bit_main` exit), not a caller of this function, and both are
// intentionally the same one-line body: there is no other way to end a
// Windows process from user code.
//
// Masked to a byte (`& 255`), matching `../linux/os.bit`'s
// `syscall(sysExitGroup(), i64(code & 255))` and `../darwin/os.bit`'s
// `_exit(i32(code & 255))` exactly: Windows' own `ExitProcess` accepts a full
// 32-bit `UINT`, but a Bit program's exit code must behave identically
// however it is compiled — the mask is what makes `main(): int { return
// 300 }` exit the same low byte on every target, not a Windows limitation.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_os_exit") fn rootExit(code: int) {
  ExitProcess(i32(code & 255))
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The UTF-16 / UTF-8 boundary (see the header above for why this is the one
// place it lives)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Encode Unicode scalar value `cp` (0..0x10FFFF, never a surrogate — callers
// substitute U+FFFD for those) as UTF-8 at `dst`, returning the byte count
// (1-4). Standard UTF-8 bit-packing, no lookup table needed.
@nosplit fn winEncodeUtf8Cp(dst: int, cp: int): int {
  if (cp <= 127) {
    storeByte(dst, cp)
    return 1
  }
  if (cp <= 2047) {
    storeByte(dst, 192 | (cp >> 6))
    storeByte(dst + 1, 128 | (cp & 63))
    return 2
  }
  if (cp <= 65535) {
    storeByte(dst, 224 | (cp >> 12))
    storeByte(dst + 1, 128 | ((cp >> 6) & 63))
    storeByte(dst + 2, 128 | (cp & 63))
    return 3
  }
  storeByte(dst, 240 | (cp >> 18))
  storeByte(dst + 1, 128 | ((cp >> 12) & 63))
  storeByte(dst + 2, 128 | ((cp >> 6) & 63))
  storeByte(dst + 3, 128 | (cp & 63))
  return 4
}

// Decode up to `maxUnits` UTF-16LE code units at raw address `src` (stopping
// early at a NUL unit) into a NUL-terminated UTF-8 byte sequence at `dst`,
// never writing past `maxBytes` (Power of 10 rules 2/3). Returns the UTF-8
// byte count written (excluding the terminating NUL), or `0 - 1` if the
// decoded text would not fit `maxBytes` — REJECTED rather than truncated,
// same choice `../os.bit`'s `osBuildRunArgv` makes for an oversized argv
// (#2023): a silently truncated argument or environment value is worse than
// an absent one, and the caller can see the drop in a lower-than-expected
// count.
//
// A high surrogate (0xD800-0xDBFF) immediately followed by a low surrogate
// (0xDC00-0xDFFF) combines into one astral-plane code point, standard
// surrogate-pair math; any other surrogate unit (unpaired, or a low
// surrogate with no preceding high) decodes as U+FFFD — see the header for
// why that substitution, not a hard failure, is the right call here.
@nosplit fn winUtf16ToUtf8(src: int, maxUnits: int, dst: int, maxBytes: int): int {
  let i = 0
  let o = 0
  while (i < maxUnits) {
    let unit = loadByte(src + i * 2) | (loadByte(src + i * 2 + 1) << 8)
    if (unit == 0) {
      break
    }
    i = i + 1
    let cp = unit
    if (unit >= 55296 && unit <= 56319) {
      // high surrogate (0xD800-0xDBFF)
      let haveLow = false
      let low = 0
      if (i < maxUnits) {
        low = loadByte(src + i * 2) | (loadByte(src + i * 2 + 1) << 8)
        if (low >= 56320 && low <= 57343) {
          haveLow = true
        }
      }
      if (haveLow) {
        cp = 65536 + ((unit - 55296) << 10) + (low - 56320)
        i = i + 1
      } else {
        cp = 65533 // U+FFFD
      }
    } else {
      if (unit >= 56320 && unit <= 57343) {
        cp = 65533 // unpaired low surrogate (0xDC00-0xDFFF)
      }
    }
    let need = 1
    if (cp > 127) {
      need = 2
    }
    if (cp > 2047) {
      need = 3
    }
    if (cp > 65535) {
      need = 4
    }
    if (o + need > maxBytes) {
      return 0 - 1
    }
    o = o + winEncodeUtf8Cp(dst + o, cp)
  }
  if (o + 1 > maxBytes) {
    return 0 - 1
  }
  storeByte(dst + o, 0)
  return o
}

// Encode the `srcLen` UTF-8 bytes at raw address `src` (a Bit `string`'s own
// bytes, so ALWAYS well-formed UTF-8 — SPEC's `string` invariant, never
// re-validated here) as NUL-terminated UTF-16LE code units at `dst`, never
// writing past `maxUnits` (Power of 10 rules 2/3). Returns the unit count
// written (excluding the terminating NUL unit), or `0 - 1` if the result
// would not fit `maxUnits` — REJECTED, never truncated, the identical
// contract `winUtf16ToUtf8` above already applies in the other direction:
// callers reject an oversized path rather than open/read/write against a
// silently shortened one.
//
// A codepoint above U+FFFF (astral plane, 4-byte UTF-8) encodes as a
// surrogate PAIR (2 units); everything else is 1 unit. Since a Bit `string`
// is always well-formed UTF-8, this can never need `winUtf16ToUtf8`'s
// U+FFFD-substitution branch — there is no such thing as a malformed
// *source* sequence here, only a destination that might not have room.
//
// BOUNDED (Power of 10 rule 2): the outer loop consumes at least 1 source
// byte per iteration (`n` below is always >= 1) and is capped by `srcLen`;
// the inner continuation-byte loop is capped by `n` (<=4) and by `srcLen`.
@nosplit fn winUtf8ToUtf16(src: int, srcLen: int, dst: int, maxUnits: int): int {
  let i = 0
  let o = 0
  while (i < srcLen) {
    let b0 = loadByte(src + i)
    let cp = b0
    let n = 1
    if ((b0 & 224) == 192) {
      cp = b0 & 31
      n = 2
    } else if ((b0 & 240) == 224) {
      cp = b0 & 15
      n = 3
    } else if ((b0 & 248) == 240) {
      cp = b0 & 7
      n = 4
    }
    let k = 1
    while (k < n && i + k < srcLen) {
      cp = (cp << 6) | (loadByte(src + i + k) & 63)
      k = k + 1
    }
    i = i + n
    if (cp > 65535) {
      if (o + 2 > maxUnits) {
        return 0 - 1
      }
      let c2 = cp - 65536
      let high = 55296 + (c2 >> 10)
      let low = 56320 + (c2 & 1023)
      storeByte(dst + o * 2, high & 255)
      storeByte(dst + o * 2 + 1, (high >> 8) & 255)
      o = o + 1
      storeByte(dst + o * 2, low & 255)
      storeByte(dst + o * 2 + 1, (low >> 8) & 255)
      o = o + 1
    } else {
      if (o + 1 > maxUnits) {
        return 0 - 1
      }
      storeByte(dst + o * 2, cp & 255)
      storeByte(dst + o * 2 + 1, (cp >> 8) & 255)
      o = o + 1
    }
  }
  if (o + 1 > maxUnits) {
    return 0 - 1
  }
  storeByte(dst + o * 2, 0)
  storeByte(dst + o * 2 + 1, 0)
  return o
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// command line -> argv
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// `GetCommandLineW`'s own documented ceiling for `CreateProcess`'s
// `lpCommandLine` (~32,767 characters plus the terminating NUL). Used here
// purely as a defensive scan bound (Power of 10 rule 2), not as a proof of
// anything Windows enforces: if this process's own command line was somehow
// assembled longer than this, the scan simply stops early and the trailing
// text is unavailable — a bounded degradation, never a fault.
const winMaxCmdUnits: int = 32768

// Every captured argument consumes at least 2 units (1 content unit + 1
// delimiter, or 1 content unit at end of string), so this count is the
// tightest bound `winMaxCmdUnits` allows — UNREACHABLE in practice, not an
// arbitrary small cap.
const winMaxArgs: int = 16384

// Worst-case UTF-8 expansion of the whole command line (3 bytes per BMP
// UTF-16 unit is the maximum single-unit expansion; a surrogate pair is 2
// units -> 4 bytes, i.e. LESS than 3x per unit), plus one NUL per argument,
// rounded up.
const winArgvArenaBytes: int = 131072

// One reusable scratch buffer for the CURRENT argument's UNESCAPED UTF-16
// units (quote/backslash processing can only shrink or preserve length,
// never grow it, so sizing this to the whole command line's own unit budget
// covers any single argument). Reused across arguments — bump-written and
// re-read within one `winSplitArgs` call, nothing here survives past it.
// `winArgvPtrs`/`winArgvArena` DO survive: they are read for the rest of the
// process's life via `../os.bit`'s `rtOsArgAt`. All are boot-time-only,
// filled ONCE before the scheduler exists (`../boot.bit`'s `boot()` step 3,
// single-threaded), so — unlike this module's per-worker run/run_test
// scratch pattern elsewhere in this file family — no slot indirection is
// needed here at all.
let winArgUnescScratch: [8193]i64 // 32768*2 + 2 bytes, rounded up to words
let winArgvPtrs: [16384]i64       // winMaxArgs pointer slots
let winArgvArena: [16384]i64      // winArgvArenaBytes / 8

// Split the NUL-terminated UTF-16LE command line at raw address `cmdAddr`
// (`GetCommandLineW`'s own return) into UTF-8 NUL-terminated arguments per
// the header's quoting rules, writing each argument's bytes into
// `winArgvArena` and its address into `winArgvPtrs[i]`. Returns the argument
// count (`argc`, always >= 1 for any non-empty command line — argv[0] is the
// program's own path/name, exactly as POSIX argv[0] is).
//
// BOUNDED (Power of 10 rule 2): the outer loop is capped by both
// `winMaxCmdUnits` and `winMaxArgs`; the inner backslash-run and
// argument-body loops are capped by the same `winMaxCmdUnits` ceiling, since
// `i` only ever advances. An argument whose decode does not fit the
// remaining `winArgvArena` budget (`winUtf16ToUtf8` returned `0 - 1`) is
// DROPPED, not truncated — the same "reject, never truncate" choice
// `osBuildRunArgv` makes; unlike that function this one cannot reject the
// WHOLE call, since a program must always get SOME argv.
@nosplit fn winSplitArgs(cmdAddr: int): int {
  let argvPtrs = int(ptrOf(winArgvPtrs))
  let arena = int(ptrOf(winArgvArena))
  let scratch = int(ptrOf(winArgUnescScratch))
  let used = 0
  let argc = 0
  let i = 0
  while (i < winMaxCmdUnits && argc < winMaxArgs) {
    while (i < winMaxCmdUnits) {
      let sep = loadByte(cmdAddr + i * 2) | (loadByte(cmdAddr + i * 2 + 1) << 8)
      if (sep != 32 && sep != 9) {
        break
      }
      i = i + 1
    }
    if (i >= winMaxCmdUnits) {
      break
    }
    let peek = loadByte(cmdAddr + i * 2) | (loadByte(cmdAddr + i * 2 + 1) << 8)
    if (peek == 0) {
      break
    }

    let outUnits = 0
    let inQuotes = false
    let done = false
    while (!done && i < winMaxCmdUnits) {
      let u = loadByte(cmdAddr + i * 2) | (loadByte(cmdAddr + i * 2 + 1) << 8)
      if (u == 0) {
        done = true
      } else {
        if (u == 92) {
          // A run of backslashes: only special when immediately followed by
          // a `"` (rule 3); otherwise every one of them is literal, in full
          // — NOT halved. See the header for why an earlier draft of this
          // function got exactly this branch wrong.
          let bs = 0
          while (i < winMaxCmdUnits &&
            (loadByte(cmdAddr + i * 2) | (loadByte(cmdAddr + i * 2 + 1) << 8)) == 92) {
            bs = bs + 1
            i = i + 1
          }
          let next = 0
          if (i < winMaxCmdUnits) {
            next = loadByte(cmdAddr + i * 2) | (loadByte(cmdAddr + i * 2 + 1) << 8)
          }
          if (next == 34) {
            let pairs = bs / 2
            let k = 0
            while (k < pairs) {
              storeByte(scratch + outUnits * 2, 92)
              storeByte(scratch + outUnits * 2 + 1, 0)
              outUnits = outUnits + 1
              k = k + 1
            }
            if (bs % 2 == 1) {
              storeByte(scratch + outUnits * 2, 34)
              storeByte(scratch + outUnits * 2 + 1, 0)
              outUnits = outUnits + 1
              i = i + 1
            }
            // else: the `"` is left unconsumed here and handled by the
            // ordinary quote-toggle branch on the next iteration — an even
            // backslash run before a quote does not escape it (rule 3).
          } else {
            let k = 0
            while (k < bs) {
              storeByte(scratch + outUnits * 2, 92)
              storeByte(scratch + outUnits * 2 + 1, 0)
              outUnits = outUnits + 1
              k = k + 1
            }
          }
        } else {
          if (u == 34) {
            inQuotes = !inQuotes
            i = i + 1
          } else {
            if (!inQuotes && (u == 32 || u == 9)) {
              done = true
            } else {
              storeByte(scratch + outUnits * 2, u & 255)
              storeByte(scratch + outUnits * 2 + 1, (u >> 8) & 255)
              outUnits = outUnits + 1
              i = i + 1
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
    storeByte(scratch + outUnits * 2, 0)
    storeByte(scratch + outUnits * 2 + 1, 0)

    let dst = arena + used
    let n = winUtf16ToUtf8(scratch, outUnits, dst, winArgvArenaBytes - used)
    if (n >= 0) {
      storeWord(argvPtrs + argc * 8, dst)
      used = used + n + 1
      argc = argc + 1
    }
  }
  return argc
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// environment -> envp
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Defensive scan ceilings (Power of 10 rule 2) for
// `GetEnvironmentStringsW`'s double-NUL-terminated block — not documented
// Windows limits (unlike `winMaxCmdUnits`, there is no equivalently strict
// published ceiling for a process's own environment block), just generous
// enough that no real process environment gets anywhere close: a malformed
// or adversarial block that never reaches a double-NUL is bounded, not
// looped forever.
const winMaxEnvUnits: int = 1048576
const winMaxEnvVars: int = 4096
const winEnvArenaBytes: int = 3145728 // 3 * winMaxEnvUnits, UTF-8 worst case

let winEnvPtrs: [4097]i64 // winMaxEnvVars + 1 (trailing 0 sentinel — ../os.bit's
// `rtOsEnv` walks this array to a 0 entry, unlike argv
// which is bounded by `argc`; see ../os.bit's own
// `osEnvp`/`rtOsEnv` header)
let winEnvArena: [393216]i64 // winEnvArenaBytes / 8

// Capture the process environment via `GetEnvironmentStringsW`, decode each
// `NAME=VALUE` entry (no un-escaping needed — unlike argv, environment
// entries carry no quoting) into `winEnvArena`, and return the address of a
// NUL-pointer-terminated array of entry addresses shaped exactly like
// `../os.bit`'s `gEnvpPtr` expects. Frees the wide-string block with
// `FreeEnvironmentStringsW` before returning, per that API's own contract.
//
// BOUNDED (Power of 10 rule 2): both the entry-length scan and the outer
// entry-count loop are capped by `winMaxEnvUnits`, and the outer loop is
// additionally capped by `winMaxEnvVars`. A `GetEnvironmentStringsW` failure
// (NULL, e.g. out of memory) yields an empty environment (a lone 0-sentinel
// entry) rather than faulting — the same "no overrides set" degradation
// `../os.bit`'s own contract already documents for a wrapper called before
// boot. An entry whose decode does not fit the remaining arena budget is
// dropped, matching `winSplitArgs`'s own choice for an oversized argument.
@nosplit fn winBuildEnvp(): int {
  let ptrs = int(ptrOf(winEnvPtrs))
  let arena = int(ptrOf(winEnvArena))
  let block: *byte = GetEnvironmentStringsW()
  let blockAddr = int(block)
  let count = 0
  if (blockAddr != 0) {
    let i = 0
    let used = 0
    while (i < winMaxEnvUnits && count < winMaxEnvVars) {
      let entryStart = i
      let entryLen = 0
      while (i < winMaxEnvUnits) {
        let u = loadByte(blockAddr + i * 2) | (loadByte(blockAddr + i * 2 + 1) << 8)
        if (u == 0) {
          break
        }
        i = i + 1
        entryLen = entryLen + 1
      }
      if (entryLen == 0) {
        break // the block's own double-NUL terminator
      }
      i = i + 1 // skip this entry's own NUL, on to the next
      let dst = arena + used
      let n = winUtf16ToUtf8(blockAddr + entryStart * 2, entryLen, dst, winEnvArenaBytes - used)
      if (n >= 0) {
        storeWord(ptrs + count * 8, dst)
        used = used + n + 1
        count = count + 1
      }
    }
    let _ = FreeEnvironmentStringsW(block)
  }
  storeWord(ptrs + count * 8, 0)
  return ptrs
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// boot-time capture entry point — called once from ../boot.bit's `boot()`
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Capture this process's real command line and environment and publish them
// through `../os.bit`'s `osSetArgs`, replacing `../boot.bit`'s old
// placeholder empty-array call. Not `export`ed: `../boot.bit` is a sibling
// file in this SAME module (`runtime/root/windows`), so it calls this
// directly with no `import` needed — the same module-scoped sharing
// `../linux/fs.bit`'s `strData`/`emptyStr` already rely on for `../linux/os.bit`.
//
// A NULL `GetCommandLineW` result (not documented to ever happen, but this is
// the process's very first Bit-managed action, before anything else exists
// to fall back on) yields `argc == 0` rather than a fault — `../os.bit`'s own
// "no args / empty env before boot" contract, now the deliberate result of a
// real (if degenerate) capture rather than the old hardcoded placeholder.
@nosplit fn winCaptureArgsEnv() {
  let argvPtr = int(ptrOf(winArgvPtrs))
  let argc = 0
  let cmd: *byte = GetCommandLineW()
  let cmdAddr = int(cmd)
  if (cmdAddr != 0) {
    argc = winSplitArgs(cmdAddr)
  }
  let envp = winBuildEnvp()
  osSetArgs(argc, argvPtr, envp)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// host_target (ABI.md §19)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// `bit_rt_host_target`: the `BuildTarget` ordinal this archive was compiled for
// -- 0 `x86_64-linux`, 1 `aarch64-linux`, 2 `aarch64-macos`, 3 `x86_64-windows`,
// the enum in compiler/build.bit. See ../linux/os.bit's counterpart for the
// full argument; the short form is that the ordinal is a property of the EMIT,
// and the emit already carries both axes it needs: the OS comes from WHICH
// PROVIDER DIRECTORY this file is in, the arch from an `asm` per-arch
// immediate the backend selects at codegen.
//
// This follows ../linux/os.bit's `asm` shape rather than ../darwin/os.bit's
// plain `return 2`: Darwin's literal is safe only because `aarch64-macos` is
// the only Darwin target the compiler recognises today (an `x86_64-macos`
// target would need its own ordinal before it could need a branch there at
// all), and this file makes no such single-arch-forever claim about Windows.
//
// Only an `x64` sub-block is written -- this provider directory is selected
// for `x86_64-windows` alone, so no `arm64` payload is ever emitted or read.
// The `result` directive still names an arm64 register (`x0`, unused)
// because SPEC §11.6's grammar fixes that directive's shape independent of
// which payload sub-blocks are present; ../../gc/windows/gcthread.bit and
// ../../thread/windows/spawn.bit already do the same, for the same reason.
//
// It does NOT call `hostTarget()`: that primitive lowers to a call to this
// very symbol once #1583 drops the `_root` infix -- the pin cycle
// `./make test-rootpins` guards (../floats.bit's SEAM 6 trap). An `asm`
// immediate has no callee at all, so there is nothing for the rename to
// redirect.
//
// `@nosplit` (#1659), on PROOF rather than assertion: §11.6 guarantees an
// `asm` block's payload is emitted inline verbatim, and the payload here is
// one immediate move -- no call, no allocation, no safepoint site. See
// ../linux/os.bit's counterpart for why `hostTarget()` itself stays refused
// inside `@nosplit`: it is an `ir.RtFn`, and every other form admitted by
// §10.3.1's allowlist is there because it is NOT a call.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_host_target") fn rtHostTarget(): int {
  return asm {
    x64 { 0xB8, 0x03, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00 } // mov eax, 3 -> x86_64-windows
    result arm64 x0 x64 rax : int
  }
}

